KEYWORDS: Artificial Intelligence; Cybersecurity; Ethical Hacking; Large Language Models; Penetration Testing; Secure Shell; Vulnerability Assessment; Ability testing; Computer operating systems; Cybersecurity; Network security; Open source software; Open systems; Software testing; Command generation; Cyber security; Ethical hacking; Language model; Large language model; Open-source; Penetration testing; Secure shell; Target systems; Vulnerability assessments; Artificial intelligence
ABSTRACT: Penetration testing (also known as Pentesting) is a systematic process that involves the identification and exploitation of vulnerabilities, misconfigurations and potential weaknesses in networks, applications, or hardware to assess their resilience against adverserial threats. Manual penetration testing requires extensive time and effort to identify and exploit vulnerabilities across complex and evolving systems. By generating precise and context-specific commands, Large Language Models (LLMs) can significantly enhance both speed and accuracy of penetration testing. This paper presents an agentic feedback-loop driven framework that involves open-source LLMs (e.g., CodeGemma, CodeLlama, Mistral) to generate linux shell commands for a wide range of penetration testing tasks and to provide an analysis on the lines of vulnerability assessment, explanation and the next recommended steps. The devised agentic framework connects to the target system via Secure Shell (SSH) protocol. The LLM then analyzes the natural language prompt entered by the user and generates a command to be executed on the target system. The console output on the target machine is then again fed to the LLM to provide a detailed analysis. Connecting to the target system via SSH for penetration testing offers several advantages, including secure, encrypted communication and the ability to perform tests without exposing sensitive data over the network. The paper also provides a detailed comparison between various open-source LLMs in their ability to generate various pentesting commands. CodeGemma:7b-instruct demonstrated the highest performance in command generation, achieving an average fuzz score of 62.32. To validate the effectiveness of the framework when integrated with this model, penetration testing was conducted on a website with known vulnerabilities, and the results presented showcase the model's ability to generate accurate and contextually relevant commands that successfully exploited the identified weaknesses. © 2025 IEEE.

